\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid30033 TRANSLATION
\par }\pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3002\fs28\cf9\insrsid30033\charrsid16347101 King Par\'e9k\'f1it inquired: }{
\cs3000\fs28\insrsid30033\charrsid16347101 My dear br\'e4hma\'eba, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Vi\'f1\'eb
u, being everyone's well-wisher, is equal and extremely dear to everyone. How, then, did He become partial like a common man for the sake of Indra and thus kill Indra's enemies? How can a person equal to 
everyone be partial to some and inimical toward others?
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid30033 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s537\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756 In }{\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{
\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756  (9.29) the Lord says, }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 samo 'ha\'e0 sarva-bh\'fcte\'f1u na me dve\'f1yo 'sti na priya\'f9:}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756 
 "I am equal to everyone. No one is dear to Me, nor is anyone My enemy." In the previous canto, however, it has been observed that the Lord sided with Indra by killing the demons on his account (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 hata-putr\'e4 diti\'f9 
\'e7akra-p\'e4r\'f1\'ebi-gr\'e4he\'eba vi\'f1\'ebun\'e4}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756 ). Therefore, the Lord was clearly partial to Indra, although He is the Supersoul in everyone's heart. The soul is ex
tremely dear to everyone, and similarly the Supersoul is also dear to everyone. Thus there cannot be any faulty action on the part of the Supersoul. The Lord is always kind to all living entities, irrespective of form and situation, yet He took the side o
f Indra just like an ordinary friend. This was the subject of Par\'e9k\'f1it Mah\'e4r\'e4ja's inquiry. As a devotee of Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba, he knew very well that K\'e5\'f1\'eba cannot be partial to anyone, but when he saw that K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a acted as the enemy of the demons, he was somewhat doubtful. Therefore he posed this question to \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 for a clear answer.
\par }\pard\plain \s908\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3007\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid11471083 A devotee cannot accept that Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u has material qualifications. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it knew perfectly well that Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, being transcendental, has nothing to do with material qualities, but to confirm his conviction he wanted to hear from the authority \'c7ukadeva Gosv
\'e4m\'e9. \'c7r\'e9la Vi\'e7van\'e4tha Cakravart\'e9 \'d6h\'e4kura says, }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 samasya katha\'e0 vai\'f1amyam:}{\cs3007\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid11471083 
 since the Lord is equally disposed toward everyone, how can He be partial? }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Priyasya katham asure\'f1u pr\'e9ty-abh\'e4va\'f9.}{\cs3007\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid11471083 
 The Lord, being the Supersoul, is extremely dear to everyone. Why, then, should the Lord display unsympathetic behavior toward the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 asuras}{\cs3007\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid11471083 ? How is this impartial? }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Suh\'e5da\'e7 ca katha\'e0 te\'f1v asauh\'e4rdam.}{\cs3007\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid11471083  Since the Lord says that He is }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 suh\'e5da\'e0 sarva-bh\'fct\'e4n\'e4m,}{
\cs3007\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid11471083  the well-wisher of all living entities, how could He act with partiality by killing demons? These questions arose in the heart of Par\'e9k\'f1it Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, and therefore he inquired from \'c7
ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9.
