\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756  king was generally the husband of many wives, and after the death of the king, especially in the battlefield, all the queens would agree to accept }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 saha-m\'e4ra\'eba,}{
\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756  dying with the husband who was their life. When P\'e4\'eb\'f2u Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, the father of the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas, died, his two wives\emdash namely, the mother of Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira, Bh\'e9
ma and Arjuna and the mother of Nakula and Sahadeva\emdash were both ready to die in the fire with their husband. Later, after a compromise was arranged, Kunt\'e9 stayed alive to care for the little children, and the other wife, M\'e4dr\'e9
, was allowed to die with her husband. This system of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 saha-m\'e4ra\'eba}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756  continued in India even until the time of British rule, but later it was di
scouraged, since the attitude of wives gradually changed with the advancement of Kali-yuga. Thus the system of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 saha-m\'e4ra\'eba}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756 
 has practically been abolished. Nevertheless, within the past fifty years I have seen the wife of a medical practitioner voluntarily 
accept death immediately when her husband died. Both the husband and wife were taken in procession in the mourning cart. Such intense love of a chaste wife for her husband is a special case.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.2.35
