wing this, why do they uselessly lament?
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid30033 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s537\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756 The Lord says in }{\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4
}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756  (2.28):
\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa60\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 avyakt\'e4d\'e9ni bh\'fct\'e4ni\line vyakta-madhy\'e4ni bh\'e4rata\line avyakta-nidhan\'e4
ny eva\line tatra k\'e4 paridevan\'e4
\par }\pard\plain \s135\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 "All created beings are unmanifest in their beginning, manifest in their interim st
ate, and unmanifest again when they are annihilated. So what need is there for lamentation?"
\par }\pard\plain \s3005\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11471083 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 
Accepting that there are two classes of philosophers, one believing in the existence of the soul and the other not believing in its existence, there is no cause fo
r lamentation in either case. Nonbelievers in the existence of the soul are called atheists by followers of Vedic wisdom. Yet even if for argument's sake we accept the atheistic theory, there is still no cause for lamentation. Apart from the separate exis
t
ence of the soul, the material elements remain unmanifested before creation. From this subtle state of unmanifestation comes manifestation, just as from ether, air is generated; from air, fire is generated; from fire, water is generated; and from water, e
a
rth becomes manifested. From the earth, many varieties of manifestations take place. For example, a big skyscraper is manifested from the earth. When it is dismantled, the manifestation becomes again unmanifested and remains as atoms in the ultimate stage
. The law of conservation of energy remains, but in the course of time things are manifested and unmanifested\emdash 
that is the difference. Then what cause is there for lamentation, in either manifestation or unmanifestation? Somehow or other, even in the unmanif
ested stage, things are not lost. Both at the beginning and at the end, all elements remain unmanifested, and this does not make any real material difference.
\par If we accept the Vedic conclusion as stated in the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\insrsid30033\charrsid11471083  (}{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 antavanta ime deh\'e4\'f9}{\insrsid30033\charrsid11471083 ) that these
 material bodies are perishable in due course of time (}{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 nityasyokt\'e4\'f9 \'e7ar\'e9ri\'eba\'f9}{\insrsid30033 
) but that the soul is eternal, then we must remember always that the body is like a dress; therefore why lament the changing of a dress? The material body has no factual existenc
e in relation to the eternal soul. It is something like a dream. In a dream we may think of flying in the sky or sitting on a chariot as a king, but when we wake up we can see that we are neither in the sky nor seated on the chariot. The Vedic wisdom enco
u
rages self-realization on the basis of the nonexistence of the material body. Therefore, in either case, whether one believes in the existence of the soul or one does not believe in the existence of the soul, there is no cause for lamentation for loss of 
the body.
\par In the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 Mah\'e4bh\'e4rata}{\insrsid30033\charrsid11471083  it is said, }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 adar\'e7an\'e4d ih\'e4y\'e4ta\'f9 puna\'e7 c\'e4dar\'e7ana\'e0 gata\'f9.}{\insrsid30033\charrsid11471083 
 This statement could support the theory of the atheistic scientist that the child in the womb of the mother has no life but is simply a lump of matter. To follow this theory
, if the lump of matter is aborted by a surgical operation, no life is killed; the body of a child is like a tumor, and if a tumor is operated upon and thrown away, no sin is involved. The same argument could be put forward in regard to the King and his q
ueens. The body of the King was manifested from an unmanifested source, and again it became unmanifested from manifestation. Since the manifestation exists only in the middle\emdash between the two points of unmanifestation\emdash 
why should one cry for the body manifested in the interim?
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.2.38
\par }\pard\plain \s2997\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11368625 \b\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 TEXT 38
