of the King of the demons; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 v\'e4kyam}{\cs2996\fs28\insrsid30033\charrsid11368625 \emdash the speech; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 diti\'f9}{\cs2996\fs28\insrsid30033\charrsid11368625 \emdash Diti, the mother of Hira\'ebyaka
\'e7ipu and Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 \'e4kar\'ebya}{\cs2996\fs28\insrsid30033\charrsid11368625 \emdash hearing; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 sa-snu\'f1\'e4}{\cs2996\fs28\insrsid30033\charrsid11368625 \emdash 
with the wife of Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 putra-\'e7okam}{\cs2996\fs28\insrsid30033\charrsid11368625 \emdash the great bereavement for her son, Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 k\'f1a\'eb\'e4t}{
\cs2996\fs28\insrsid30033\charrsid11368625 \emdash immediately; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 tyaktv\'e4}{\cs2996\fs28\insrsid30033\charrsid11368625 \emdash giving up; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 tattve}{\cs2996\fs28\insrsid30033\charrsid11368625 
\emdash in the real philosophy of life; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 cittam}{\cs2996\fs28\insrsid30033\charrsid11368625 \emdash heart; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 adh\'e4rayat}{\cs2996\fs28\insrsid30033\charrsid11368625 \emdash engaged.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid30033 TRANSLATION
\par }\pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3002\fs28\cf9\insrsid30033\charrsid16347101 \'c7r\'e9 N\'e4rada Muni continued:}{
\cs3000\fs28\insrsid30033\charrsid16347101  Diti, the mother of Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu and Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a, heard the instructions of Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu along with her daughter-in-law, Ru\'f1\'e4bh\'e4nu, Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1
a's wife. She then forgot her grief over her son's death and thus engaged her mind and attention in understanding the real philosophy of life.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid30033 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s537\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756 
When a relative dies one certainly becomes very much interested in philosophy, but when the funeral ceremony is over one again becomes attentive to ma
terialism. Even Daityas, who are materialistic persons, sometimes think of philosophy when some relative meets death. The technical term for this attitude of the materialistic person is }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'e7ma\'e7\'e4na-vair\'e4gya,}{
\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756  or detachment in a cemetery or place of cremation. As confirmed in }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4,}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756 
 four classes of men receive an understanding of spiritual life and God\emdash }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'e4rta}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756  (the distressed), }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 jij\'ef\'e4su}{
\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756  (the inquisitive), }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 arth\'e4rth\'e9}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756  (one who desires material gains) and }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 j\'ef\'e4n\'e9}{
\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756  (one who is searching for knowledge). Especially when one is very much distressed by material conditions, one becomes interested in God. Therefore Kunt\'e9dev\'e9 said in her prayers to K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a that she preferred distress to a happy mood of life. In the material world, one who is happy forgets K\'e5\'f1\'eba, or God, but sometimes, if one is actually pious but in distress, he remembers K\'e5\'f1\'eba. Queen Kunt\'e9dev\'e9
 therefore preferred distress because it is an opportunity for remembering K\'e5\'f1\'eba. When K\'e5\'f1\'eba was leaving Kunt\'e9dev\'e9 for His own country, Kunt\'e9dev\'e9 regretfully said that she was better off in distress because K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a was always present, whereas now that the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas were situated in their kingdom, K\'e5\'f1\'eba was going away. For a devotee, distress is an opportunity to remember the Supreme Personality of Godhead constantly.
\par }\pard\plain \s1178\ql \li0\ri0\sb360\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Thus end the Bhaktivedanta purports of the Seventh Canto, Second Chapter, of the }{
\cs2344\i0\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  entitled "Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu, King of the Demons."
\par }\pard\plain \s20\qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf18\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.3: Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu's Plan to Become Immortal
\par }\pard\plain \s330\qc \li0\ri0\sb540\sa540\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat10 \b\f35\fs36\cf8\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\insrsid10039258 CHAPTER THREE\line }{\insrsid30033 Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu's Plan to Become Immortal
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.3 Summary
\par }\pard\plain \qj \fi360\li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 This chapter describes how Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu performed a severe type of austerity for material benefit, thus causing great distress throughout the universe. Even Lord Brahm\'e4, the chief personality within this universe, became somewhat disturbed and personally went to see why Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu was engaged in such a severe austerity.
\par Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu wanted to become immortal. He wanted not to be conquered by anyone, not to be attacked by old age and disease, and not to be harassed by any opponent. Thus he wanted to beco
me the absolute ruler of the entire universe. With this desire, he entered the valley of Mandara Mountain and began practicing a severe type of austerity and meditation. Seeing Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu engaged in this austerity, the demigods returned to their respective homes, but while Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu was thus engaged, a kind of fire began blazing from his head, disturbing the entire universe and its inhabitants, including the birds, beasts and demigods. When all the higher and lower planets became too hot to live on, 
the demigods, being disturbed, left their abodes in the higher planets and went to see Lord Brahm\'e4, praying to him that he curtail this unnecessary heat. The demigods disclosed to Lord Brahm\'e4 Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu's ambition to become immortal, overcoming his short duration of life, and to be the master of all the planetary systems, even Dhruvaloka.
\par Upon hearing about the purpose of Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu's austere meditation, Lord Brahm\'e4, accompanied by the great sage Bh\'e5gu and great personalities like Dak\'f1a, went to see Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu. He then sprinkled water from his }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 kama\'eb\'f2alu,}{\insrsid30033  a type of waterpot, upon Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu's head.
\par Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu, the King of the Daityas, bowed down before Lord Brahm\'e4, the creator of this universe, offering respectful obeisances again and again and offering prayers. When Lord Brahm\'e4
 agreed to give him benedictions, he prayed not be killed by any living entity, not to be killed in any place, covered or uncovered, not to die in the daytime or at night, not to be killed by any weapon, on land or in the
 air, and not to be killed by any human being, animal, demigod or any other entity, living or nonliving. He further prayed for supremacy over the entire universe and requested the eight yogic perfections, such as }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 a\'ebim\'e4}{
\insrsid30033  and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 laghim\'e4.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033\charrsid2446254 SB 7.3.1
