\insrsid10039258 CHAPTER FIVE\line }{\insrsid30033 Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, the Saintly Son of Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.5 Summary
\par }\pard\plain \qj \fi360\li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja did not carry out the orders of his teachers, for he was always engaged in worshiping Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu. As described in this chapter, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu tried to kill Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja, even by having a snake bite him and by putting him under the feet of elephants, yet he was unsuccessful.
\par Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu's spiritual master, \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, had two sons named \'d1a\'eb\'f2a and Amarka, to whom Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja was entrusted for education. Although the teachers tried to educate the boy Prahl\'e4
da in politics, economics and other material activities, he did not care for their instructions. Instead, he continued to be a pure devotee. Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja never liked 
the idea of discriminating between one's friends and enemies. Because he was spiritually inclined, he was equal toward everyone.
\par Once upon a time, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu inquired from his son what the best thing was that he had learned from his teachers. Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja replied that a man engrossed in the material consciousness of duality, thinking, "This is mine, and that belongs to my enemy," should give up his householder life and go to the forest to worship the Supreme Lord.
\par When Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu heard from his son about devotional service, he decided that this small boy had been polluted by some friend in school. Thus he advised the teachers to take care of the boy so that he would not become a K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a conscious devotee. However, when the teachers inquired from Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja why he was going against their teachings, Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja taught the teachers that the mentality of ownership is false and that he was therefore trying to become an unalloyed devotee of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu. The teachers, being very angry at this an
swer, chastised and threatened the boy with many fearful conditions. They taught him to the best of their ability and then brought him before his father.
\par Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu affectionately took his son Prahl\'e4da on his lap and then inquired from him what the best thing was that he had learned from his teachers. As usual, Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja began praising the nine processes of devotional service, such as }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'e7rava\'ebam}{\insrsid30033  and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 k\'e9rtanam.}{\insrsid30033  Thus the King of the demons, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu, being extremely angry, chastised the teachers, \'d1a\'eb\'f2a and Amarka, for having wrongly trained Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja. The so-called teachers informed the King that Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja was automatically a devotee and did not listen to their instructions. When they proved themselves innocent, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu inquired from Prahl\'e4da where he had learned }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 vi\'f1\'ebu-bhakti.}{\insrsid30033  Prahl\'e4da Mah
\'e4r\'e4ja replied that those who are attached to family life do not develop K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness, either personally or collectively. Instead, they suffer repeated birth and death in this material 
world and continue simply chewing the chewed. Prahl\'e4da explained that the duty of every man is to take shelter of a pure devotee and thus become eligible to understand K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness.
\par Enraged at this answer, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu threw Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja from his lap. Since Prahl\'e4da was so treacherous that he had become a devotee of Vi\'f1\'ebu, who had killed his uncle Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu asked his assistants to kill him. The assistants of Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu struck Prahl\'e4da with sharp weapons, threw him under the f
eet of elephants, subjected him to hellish conditions, threw him from the peak of a mountain and tried to kill him in thousands of other ways, but they were unsuccessful. Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu therefore became increasingly afraid of his son Prahl\'e4da Mah
\'e4r\'e4ja and arrested him. The sons of Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu's spiritual master, \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, began teaching Prahl\'e4da in their own way, but Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja did not accept their instructions. While the teachers were absent from the classroom, Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja began to preach K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a consciousness in the school, and by his instructions all his class friends, the sons of the demons, became devotees like him.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.5.1
