Politics involves accepting one group of men as enemies and another group as friends. Everything in politics is based on this philosophy, and the entire world, especially at the present, is engrossed in it. The public is concern
ed with friendly countries and friendly groups or enemy countries and enemy groups, but as stated in }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4,}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756 
 a learned person does not make distinctions between enemies and friends. Devotees, especially, do not create friends and enemies. A devotee sees that every living being is part and parcel of K\'e5\'f1\'eba (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 mamaiv\'e4
\'e0\'e7o j\'e9va-bh\'fcta\'f9}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756 ). Therefore a devotee treats friends and enemies equally by trying to educate them both in K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a consciousness. Of course, atheistic men do not follow the instructions of pure devotees, but instead consider a devotee their enemy. A devotee, however, never creates a situation of friendship and enmity. Although Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja was obliged to hear the instructions of \'d1a\'eb\'f2a and Amarka, he did not like the philosophy of friends and enemies, which forms the basis of politics. He was not interested in this philosophy.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.5.4
