\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 eva}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash certainly; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 loke asmin}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash in this material world; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 pu\'e0sa\'f9}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash 
of the living entity; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 sva-artha\'f9}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash the real self-interest; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 para\'f9}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash transcendental; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 sm\'e5ta\'f9}{
\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash regarded; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 ek\'e4nta-bhakti\'f9}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash unalloyed devotional service; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 govinde}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash to Govinda; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 
yat}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash which; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 sarvatra}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash everywhere; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 tat-\'e9k\'f1a\'ebam}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash seeing the relationship with Govinda, K\'e5\'f1\'eba.

\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid30033 TRANSLATION
\par }\pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\insrsid30033 
In this material world, to render service to the lotus feet of Govinda, the cause of all causes, and to see Him everywhere, is the only goal of life. This much alone is the ultimate goal of human life, as explained by all the revealed scriptures.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid30033 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s537\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756 In this verse the words }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
sarvatra tad-\'e9k\'f1a\'ebam}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033\charrsid14425756  describe the highest perfection of devotional service, in which one sees everything with reference to Govinda's activities. The highly elevated d
evotee never sees anything unrelated to Govinda.
\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa60\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 sth\'e4vara-ja\'ecgama dekhe, n\'e4 dekhe t\'e4ra m\'fcrti\line sarvatra haya nija i\'f1
\'f6a-deva-sph\'fcrti
\par }\pard\plain \s135\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 "The }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 mah\'e4-bh\'e4gavata,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 the advanced devotee, certainly sees everything mobile and immobile, but he does not exactly see their forms. Rather, everywhere he immediately sees manifest the form of the Supreme Lord." (Cc. }{\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Madhya}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  8.274) Even in this material world, a devotee does not see materially manifested things; instead he sees Govinda in everything. When he sees a tree or a human being, a devotee sees them in relation to Govinda. }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Govindam \'e4di-puru\'f1am:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  Govinda is the original source of everything.
\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa60\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'e9\'e7vara\'f9 parama\'f9 k\'e5\'f1\'eba\'f9\line sac-cid-\'e4nanda-vigraha\'f9\line an
\'e4dir \'e4dir govinda\'f9\line sarva-k\'e4ra\'eba-k\'e4ra\'ebam
\par }\pard\plain \s135\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 "K\'e5\'f1\'eba, who is known as Govinda, is the supreme c
ontroller. He has an eternal, blissful, spiritual body. He is the origin of all. He has no other origin, for He is the prime cause of all causes." (}{\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid3828919 Brahma-sa\'e0hit\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 5.1) The test of a perfect devotee is that he sees Govinda everywhere in this universe, even in every atomic particle (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 a\'eb\'f2a-ntara-stha-param\'e4\'ebu-cay\'e4ntara-stham}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
). This is the perfect vision of a devotee. It is therefore said:
\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa60\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 n\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba-maya\'e0 dh\'e9r\'e4\'f9\line pa\'e7yanti param\'e4rthina\'f9\line 
jagad dhana-maya\'e0 lubdh\'e4\'f9\line k\'e4muk\'e4\'f9 k\'e4min\'e9mayam
\par }\pard\plain \s135\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 A devotee sees everyone and everything in relationship with N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba (}{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 n\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba mayam}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 ). Everything is an expansion of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a's energy. Just as those who are greedy see everything as a source of money-making and those who are lusty see everything as being conducive to sex, the most perfect devotee, Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, saw N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a even within a stone column. This does not mean, however, that we must accept the words }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 daridra-n\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 which have been manufactured by some unscrupulous person. One who actually envisions N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba everywhere makes no distinction between the poor and the rich. To single out the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 daridra-n\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba,}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  or poor N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, and reject the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 dhani-n\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  or rich N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a, is not the vision of a devotee. Rather, that is the imperfect vision of materialistic persons.
\par }\pard\plain \s1178\ql \li0\ri0\sb360\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Thus end the Bhaktivedanta purports of the Seventh Canto, Seventh Chapter, of the }{
\cs2344\i0\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  entitled "What Prahl\'e4da Learned in the Womb."
\par }\pard\plain \s20\qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf18\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.8: Lord }{\insrsid3609589 N\'e5si\'e0hadeva}{\insrsid30033  Slays the King of the Demons
\par }\pard\plain \s330\qc \li0\ri0\sb540\sa540\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat10 \b\f35\fs36\cf8\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\insrsid8789409 CHAPTER EIGHT\line }{\insrsid30033 Lord }{\insrsid10038355 N\'e5si\'e0hadeva}{\insrsid30033  Slays the King of the Demons
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.8 Summary
\par }\pard\plain \qj \fi360\li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 As described in this chapter, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu was ready to kill his own son Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja, but the Supreme Personality of Godhead appeared in front of the demon as \'c7r\'e9 N\'e5ke\'e7ar\'e9, half lion and half man, and killed him.
\par Following the instructions of Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, all the sons of the demons became attached to Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Supreme Personality of Godhead. When this attachment became pronounced, their teachers, \'d1a\'eb\'f2
a and Amarka, were very much afraid that the boys would become more and more devoted to the Lord. In a helpless condition, they approached Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu and described in detail the effect of Prahl\'e4da's preaching. After hearing of this, Hira\'eb
yaka\'e7ipu decided to kill his son Prahl\'e4da. Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu was so angry that Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja fell down at his feet and said many things just to pacify him, but he was unsuccessful in satisfying his demoniac father. Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu, as a typical demon, began to advertise himself as being greater than the Supreme Personality of Godhead, but Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja challenged him, saying that Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu was not God, and began to glorify the Supreme Personality of Godhead, declaring that the Lord is all-pervading, that everything is under Him, and that no one is equal to or greater than Him. Thus he request
ed his father to be submissive to the omnipotent Supreme Lord.
\par The more Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja glorified the Supreme Personality of Godhead, the more angry and agitated the demon became. Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu asked his Vai\'f1\'ebava son whether his God existed within the columns of the palace, and Prahl\'e4da Mah
\'e4r\'e4ja immediately accepted that since the Lord is present everywhere, He was also present within the columns. When Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu heard this philosophy from his young son, he derided the boy's statement as just the talk 
of a child and forcefully struck the pillar with his fist.
\par As soon as Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu struck the column, there issued forth a tumultuous sound. At first Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu, the King of the demons, could not see anything but the pillar, but to substantiate Prahl\'e4d
a's statements, the Lord came out of the pillar in His wonderful incarnation as Narasi\'e0ha, half lion and half man. Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu could immediately understand that the extraordinarily wonderful form of the Lord was surely meant for his death, and thus he pr
epared to fight with the form of half lion and half man. The Lord performed His pastimes by fighting with the demon for some time, and in the evening, on the border between day and night, the Lord captured the demon, threw him on His lap, and killed him b
y piercing his abdomen with His nails. The Lord not only killed Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu, the King of the demons, but also killed many of his followers. When there was no one else to fight, the Lord, roaring with anger, sat down on Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu's throne.
\par The entire universe was thus relieved of the rule of Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu, and everyone was jubilant in transcendental bliss. Then all the demigods, headed by Lord Brahm\'e4, approached the Lord. These included the great saintly persons, the Pit\'e4
s, the Siddhas, the Vidy\'e4dharas, the N\'e4gas, the Manus, the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 praj\'e4patis,}{\insrsid30033  the Gandharvas, the C\'e4ra\'ebas, the Yak\'f1as, the Kimpuru\'f1as, the Vait\'e4
likas, the Kinnaras and also many other varieties of beings in human form. All of them stood not far from the Supreme Personality of Godhead and began offering their prayers unto the Lord, whose spiritual effulgence was brilliant as He sat on the throne.

\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.8.1
\par }\pard\plain \s1167\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\insrsid30033 TEXT 1
