\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 na}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash not; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 iha}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash in this world; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 \'e7ara\'ebam}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash shelter (protection); }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 pitarau}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash the father and mother; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 n\'e5si\'e0ha}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash O my Lord }{\fs28\insrsid10038355 N\'e5si\'e0hadeva}{\fs28\insrsid30033 ; }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 na}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash neither; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 \'e4rtasya}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash of a person suffering from some disease; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 ca}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash also; }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 agadam}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash medicine; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 udanvati}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash in the water of the ocean; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 majjata\'f9}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash 
of a person who is drowning; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 nau\'f9}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash the boat; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 taptasya}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash of a person suffering from a condition of material misery; }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 tat-pratividhi\'f9}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash the counteraction (invented for stopping the suffering of material existence); }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 ya\'f9}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash that which; }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 iha}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash in this material world; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 a\'efjas\'e4}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash very easily; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 i\'f1\'f6a\'f9}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash 
accepted (as a remedy); }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 t\'e4vat}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash similarly; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 vibho}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash O my Lord, O Supreme; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 tanu-bh\'e5t\'e4m}{
\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash of the living entities who have accepted material bodies; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid30033 tvat-upek\'f1it\'e4n\'e4m}{\fs28\insrsid30033 \emdash who are neglected by You and not accepted by You.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid30033 TRANSLATION
\par }\pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\insrsid30033 My Lord }{\fs28\insrsid10038355 N\'e5si\'e0hadeva}{\fs28\insrsid30033 
, O Supreme, because of a bodily conception of life, embodied souls neglected and not cared for by You cannot do anything for their betterment. Whatever remedies they accept, although perhaps temporarily beneficial, are certai
nly impermanent. For example, a father and mother cannot protect their child, a physician and medicine cannot relieve a suffering patient, and a boat on the ocean cannot protect a drowning man.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid30033 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s537\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Through parental care, through remedies for different 
kinds of disease, and through means of protection on the water, in the air and on land, there is always an endeavor for relief from various kinds of suffering in the material world, but none of them are guaranteed measures for protection. They may be bene
f
icial temporarily, but they afford no permanent benefit. Despite the presence of a father and mother, a child cannot be protected from accidental death, disease and various other miseries. No one can help, including the parents. Ultimately the shelter is 
the Lord, and one who takes shelter of the Lord is protected. This is guaranteed. As the Lord says in }{\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  (9.31), }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 kaunteya pratij\'e4n\'e9
hi na me bhakta\'f9 pra\'eba\'e7yati:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  "O son of Kunt\'e9, declare it boldly that My devotee never perishes." Therefore, unless 
one is protected by the mercy of the Lord, no remedial measure can act effectively. One should consequently depend fully on the causeless mercy of the Lord. Although as a matter of routine duty one must of course accept other remedial measures, no one can
 
protect one who is neglected by the Supreme Personality of Godhead. In this material world, everyone is trying to counteract the onslaught of material nature, but everyone is ultimately fully controlled by material nature. Therefore even though so-called 
philosophers and scientists try to surmount the onslaught of material nature, they have not been able to do so. K\'e5\'f1\'eba says in }{\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 (13.9) that the real sufferings of the material world are four\emdash }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 janma-m\'e5tyu jar\'e4-vy\'e4dhi}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  (birth, death, old age and dis
ease). In the history of the world, no one has been successful in conquering these miseries imposed by material nature. }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11343256 P}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 rak\'e5te\'f9 kriyam\'e4\'eb\'e4ni gu\'ebai\'f9 karm\'e4\'eb
