 (7.10) the Lord says:
\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa60\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 b\'e9ja\'e0 m\'e4\'e0 sarva-bh\'fct\'e4n\'e4\'e0\line viddhi p\'e4rtha san\'e4tanam
\par }\pard\plain \s135\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 "O son of P\'e5th\'e4, know that I am t
he original seed of all existences." In the Vedic literature it is said, }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'e9\'e7\'e4v\'e4syam ida\'e0 sarvam, yato v\'e4 im\'e4ni bh\'fct\'e4ni j\'e4yante}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  and }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
sarva\'e0 khalv ida\'e0 brahma.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  All this Vedic information indicates that there is only one God and that there is nothing else but Him. The M\'e4y\'e4v\'e4d\'e9
 philosophers explain this in their own way, but the Supreme Personality of Godhead asserts the truth that He is everything and yet is separate from everything. This is the philosophy of \'c7r\'e9 Caitanya Mah\'e4prabhu, which is called }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 acintya-bhed\'e4bheda-tattva.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  Everything is one, the Supreme Lord, yet everything is separate from the Lord. This is the understanding of oneness and difference.
\par }\pard\plain \s908\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 The example given in this regard\emdash }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 vasuk\'e4lavad }{
\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 a\'f1\'f6i}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 -}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 tarvo\'f9}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \emdash is very easy to understand. Everything exists in time, yet there are different phases of the time factor\emdash 
present, past and future. Present, past and future are one. Every day we can experience the time factor as morning, noon and evening, and although morning is different from noon, which is different from 
evening, all of them taken together are one. The time factor is the energy of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, but the Lord is separate from the time factor. Everything is created, maintained and annihilated by time, but the Supreme Lord, the Personali
ty of Godhead, has no beginning and no end. He is }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 nitya\'f9 \'e7\'e4\'e7vata\'f9}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \emdash 
eternal, permanent. Everything passes through time's phases of present, past and future, yet the Lord is always the same. Thus there is undoubtedly a difference between the Lord and the cosmi
c manifestation, but actually they are not different. Accepting them to be different is called }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 avidy\'e4,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  ignorance.
\par True oneness, however, is not equivalent to the conception of the M\'e4y\'e4v\'e4d\'e9s. The true understanding is that the differences are manifested by 
the energy of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. The seed is manifested as a tree, which displays varieties in its trunk, branches, leaves, flowers and fruits. \'c7r\'e9la Bhaktivinoda \'d6h\'e4kura has therefore sung, }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
ke\'e7ava tuy\'e4 jagata vicitra:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  "My dear Lord, Your creation is full of varieties." The varieties are one and at the same time different. This is the philosophy of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 acintya-bhed\'e4bheda-tattva.}
{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  The conclusion given in }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Brahma-sa\'e0hit\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  is this:
\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa60\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'e9\'e7vara\'f9 parama\'f9 k\'e5\'f1\'eba\'f9\line sac-cid-\'e4nanda-vigraha\'f9\line an
\'e4dir \'e4dir govinda\'f9\line sarva-k\'e4ra\'eba-k\'e4ra\'ebam
\par }\pard\plain \s135\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 "K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, known as Govinda, is the supreme controller. He has an eternal, blissful, spiritual body. He is the origin of all. He has no other origin, for He is the prime cause of all causes." Because the Lord is the supreme cause, everythi
ng is one with Him, but when we consider varieties, we find that one thing is different from another.
\par }\pard\plain \s908\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
We may conclude, therefore, that there is no difference between one thing and another, yet in varieties there are differences. In this regard, Madhv\'e4c\'e4rya
 gives an example concerning a tree and a tree in fire. Both trees are the same, but they look different because of the time factor. The time factor is under the control of the Supreme Lord, and therefore the Supreme Lord is different from time. An advanc
ed devotee consequently does not distinguish between happiness and distress. As stated in }{\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  (10.14.8):
\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa60\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 tat te 'nukamp\'e4\'e0 susam\'e9k\'f1am\'e4\'ebo\line bhu\'efj\'e4na ev\'e4tma-k\'e5ta\'e0
 vip\'e4kam
\par }\pard\plain \s135\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 When a devotee is in a condition of so-called distress, he considers it a gif
t or blessing from the Supreme Personality of Godhead. When a devotee is always thus situated in K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness in any condition of life, he is described as }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 mukti-pade sa d\'e4ya-bh\'e4k,}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  a perfect candidate for returning home, back to Godhead. The word }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 d\'e4ya-bh\'e4k}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 means "inheritance." A son inherits the property of his father. Similarly, when the devotee is fully K\'e5\'f1\'eba conscious, undisturbed by dualities, he is sure that he will return home, back to Godhead, just as one inherits his father's property.

\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.9.32
\par }\pard\plain \s1167\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\insrsid30033 TEXT 32
