u, having many thousands of faces and forms. This process is called self-realization. Genuine self-realization consists not of perceiving the impersonal effulgence of the Lord, but seeing face to face the transcendental form of the Lord. As
 distinctly mentioned here, Lord Brahm\'e4 saw the Supreme Lord as }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 mah\'e4-puru\'f1a,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Arjuna also saw K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a in this same way. Therefore he told the Lord, }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 para\'e0 brahma para\'e0 dh\'e4ma pavitra\'e0 parama\'e0 bhav\'e4n puru\'f1a\'e0 \'e7\'e4\'e7vata\'e0 divyam:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  "
You are the Supreme Brahman, the ultimate, the supreme abode and purifier, the Absolute Truth and the eternal divine person." The Lord is }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 parama-puru\'f1a,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  the supreme form. }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Puru\'f1a\'e0 \'e7\'e4\'e7vatam:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  He is everlastingly the supreme enjoyer. It is not that the imperso
nal Brahman assumes a form; on the contrary, the impersonal Brahman effulgence is an emanation from the supreme form of the Lord. Upon being purified, Brahm\'e4
 could see the supreme form of the Lord. The impersonal Brahman cannot have heads, noses, ears, hands and legs. This is not possible, for these are attributes of the Lord's form.
\par }\pard\plain \s908\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 The word }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 m\'e4y\'e4mayam}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
