\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  describing the Lord's form, should not be taken to mean illusion. Rather, the Lord's form is factual, and seeing this form is the result of perfect knowledge. This is confirmed in }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Bhagavad-g\'e9t
\'e4:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 bah\'fcn\'e4\'e0 janman\'e4m ante j\'ef\'e4nav\'e4n m\'e4\'e0 prapadyate.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  The word }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 j\'ef\'e4nav\'e4n}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 refers to one who is perfectly in knowledge.
 Such a person can see the Personality of Godhead, and therefore he surrenders unto the Lord. The Lord's being symptomized by a face, nose, ears and so on is eternal. Without such a form, no one can be blissful. The Lord, however, is }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 sac-cid-\'e4nanda-vigraha,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  as stated in the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'e7\'e4stra}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'e9\'e7vara\'f9 parama\'f9}{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid8263779  k\'e5\'f1\'eba\'f9 sac-cid-\'e4nanda-vigraha\'f9}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 ). When one is in perfect transcendental bliss, he can see the Lord's supreme form (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 vigraha}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 ). In this regard, \'c7r\'e9la Madhv\'e4c\'e4rya says:
