\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid30033 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s537\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
Material achievements are not the ultimate goal of devotional service. The ultimate goal of devotional service is love of Godhead. Therefore although Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, Ambar\'e9\'f1a Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira Mah
\'e4r\'e4ja an
d many devotee kings were materially very opulent, they accepted their material opulence in the service of the Lord, not for their personal sense gratification. Of course, possessing material opulence is always fearful because under the influence of mater
ial opulence one may be misdirected from devotional service. Nonetheless, a pure devotee (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 any\'e4bhil\'e4\'f1it\'e4-\'e7\'fcnyam}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
) is never misdirected by material opulence. On the contrary, whatever he possesses he engages one hundred percent in the service of the Lord. When one is allured by material possessions, they are considered to be given by }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 m\'e4y\'e4,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  but when one uses material possessions fully for service, they are considered God's gifts, or facilities offered by K\'e5\'f1\'eba for enhancing one's devotional service.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.10.2
