ee; he always thinks himself to be the lowest, to be incompetent and unqualified. Thus after hearing the narration of Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja's activities, Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira, who was on the same standard of devotional service as Prahl
\'e4da, might have been thinking of his own humble position. N\'e4rada Muni, however, could understand Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira's mind, and therefore he immediately encouraged him by saying that the P\'e4\'eb\'f2
avas were not less fortunate; they were as good as Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja because although Lord }{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10038355 N\'e5si\'e0hadeva}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  appeared for Prahl\'e4
da, the Supreme Personality of Godhead in His original form as K\'e5\'f1\'eba was always living with the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas. Although the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas, because of the influence of K\'e5\'f1\'eba's }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 yogam\'e4y\'e4,}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  could not think of their fortunate position, every saintly person, including the great sage N\'e4rada, could understand it, and therefore they constantly visited Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira.
