\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  should receive charity, and this should be his means of livelihood. A }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  cannot take up any professional occupational duty for his livelihood. The }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'e7\'e4stras}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  especially stress that if one claims to be a }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'eba,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 he cannot engage in the service of anyone else; otherwise he at once falls from his position and becomes a }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'e7\'fcdra.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  \'c7r\'e9la R\'fcpa Gosv\'e4m\'e9 and San\'e4tana Gosv\'e4m\'e9
 belonged to a very respectful family, but because they engaged in the service of Nawab Hussain Shah\emdash not even as ordinary clerks, but as ministers\emdash 
they were ostracized from brahminical society. Indeed, they became like Mohammedans and even changed their names. Unless a }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 is very pure, he cannot accept charity from others. Charity should be given to those who are pure. Even if one is born in a family of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  if one acts as a }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'e7\'fcdra}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  one cannot accept charity, for this is strictly prohibited. Although the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 k\'f1atriyas}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  are almost as qualified as the }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  even they cannot accept charity. This is strictly prohibited in this verse by the word }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 apratigraha.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 What to speak of the lower social orders, even the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 k\'f1atriyas}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  must not accept charity. The king or government may levy taxes upon the citizens in various ways\emdash 
by revenue duties, customs duties, realization of fines, and so on\emdash provided the king is able to give full protection to his subjects to assure the security of their life and property. Unless he is able to giv
e protection, he cannot levy taxes. However, a king must not levy any tax upon the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  and the Vai\'f1\'ebavas fully engaged in K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness.
