 institution of society, and now, in this chapter, he will describe the stages of spiritual advancement in the four }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'e4\'e7ramas,}{\insrsid30033  which are known as }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 brahmacarya, g\'e5hastha, v\'e4
naprastha}{\insrsid30033  and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 sanny\'e4sa.
\par }{\insrsid30033 The }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 brahmac\'e4ri}{\insrsid30033  should live under the care of the true spiritual master, giving him sincere respect and obeisances, acting as his menial servant, and always carrying out his order. The }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 brahmac\'e4r\'e9}{\insrsid30033  should engage himself in spiritual activities and study the Vedic literature under the direction of the spiritual master. According to the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 brahmacarya}{\insrsid30033 
 system, he should dress with a belt, deerskin, and matted hair and should bear a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 da\'eb\'f2a,}{\insrsid30033 
 waterpot and sacred thread. He should collect alms daily in the morning, and in the evening whatever alms he has collected he should offer to the spiritual master. A }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 brahmac\'e4r\'e9}{\insrsid30033  should accept }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 pras\'e4da}{\insrsid30033  upon the order of the spiritual master, and if the spiritual master sometimes forgets to order the disciple to eat, the disciple should not take }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 pras\'e4da}{\insrsid30033 
 on his own initiative; rather, he should fast. The }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 brahmac\'e4r\'e9}{\insrsid30033  should be trained to be satisfie
d with eating what is absolutely necessary, he should be very expert in executing responsibilities, he should be faithful, and he should control his senses and try to avoid the association of women as far as possible. A }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 brahmac
\'e4r\'e9}{\insrsid30033  should very strictly abstain from living with women and should not meet with }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 g\'e5hasthas}{\insrsid30033  and those too addicted to women. Nor should a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 brahmac\'e4r\'e9}{
\insrsid30033  speak in a lonely place with a woman.
\par After completing one's education as a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 brahmac\'e4r\'e9}{\insrsid30033  in this way, one should give }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 dak\'f1i\'eb\'e4,}{\insrsid30033  an offering of gratitude, to one's }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 guru,}{
\insrsid30033  and then one may leave for home and accept the next }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'e4\'e7rama}{\insrsid5713184 \emdash }{\insrsid30033 the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 g\'e5hastha-\'e4\'e7rama}{\insrsid5713184\charrsid5713184 \emdash }{
\insrsid30033 or else one may continue in the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 brahmacarya-\'e4\'e7rama}{\insrsid30033  without adulteration. The duties for the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 g\'e5hastha-\'e4\'e7rama}{\insrsid30033  and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 
brahmacarya-\'e4\'e7rama,}{\insrsid30033  as well as the duties for }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 sanny\'e4s\'e9s,}{\insrsid30033  are prescribed in the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'e7\'e4stras.}{\insrsid30033  A }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 g\'e5hastha}{
\insrsid30033  is not meant to enjoy sex life without restriction. Indeed, the whole purpose of Vedic life is to become free from sexual indulgence. All the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'e4\'e7ramas}{\insrsid30033  are recognized for spiritual progress, and 
therefore although the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 g\'e5hastha-\'e4\'e7rama}{\insrsid30033  gives a kind of license for sex life for a certain time, it does not allow unrestricted sex life. Therefore, in }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 g\'e5hastha}{\insrsid30033 
 life also, there is no illicit sex. A }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 g\'e5hastha}{\insrsid30033  should not accept a woman for sexual enjoyment. Wasting semen is also illicit sex.
\par After the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 g\'e5hastha-\'e4\'e7rama}{\insrsid30033  is another }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'e4\'e7rama,}{\insrsid30033  known as }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 v\'e4naprastha,}{\insrsid30033  which is midway between }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 g\'e5hastha}{\insrsid30033  and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 sanny\'e4sa.}{\insrsid30033  A person in the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 v\'e4naprastha}{\insrsid30033 
 order is restricted in eating food grains and forbidden to eat fruits that have not ripened on the tree. Nor should he cook food with fire, although he is allowed to eat }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 caru,}{\insrsid30033 
 grains that have been offered in a sacrificial fire. He may also eat fruits and grains that have grown naturally. Living in a thatched cottage, the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 v\'e4naprastha}{\insrsid30033  
should endure all kinds of heat and cold. He should not cut his nails or hair, and he should give up cleaning his body and teeth. He should wear tree bark, accept a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 da\'eb\'f2a,}{\insrsid30033 
 and practice life in the forest, taking a vow to live there for twelve years, eight years, four years, two years or at least one year. At last, when because of old age he can no longer perform the activities of a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 v\'e4naprastha,}
{\insrsid30033  he should gradually stop everything and in this way give up his body.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.12.1
