nti [the day when the sun begins to move north] or on the Karka\'f6a-sa\'eckr\'e4nti [the day when the sun begins to move south]. One should also perform this ceremony on the Me\'f1a-sa\'eckr\'e4nti day and the Tul\'e4-sa\'eckr\'e4
nti day, in the yoga named Vyat\'e9p\'e4ta, on that day in which three lunar tithis are conjoined, during an eclipse of either the moon or the sun, on the twelfth lunar day, and in the \'c7rava\'eba-nak\'f1atra. One should perform this ceremony on the Ak
\'f1aya-t\'e5t\'e9y\'e4 day, on the ninth lunar day of the bright fortnight of the month of K\'e4rttika, on the four a\'f1\'f6ak\'e4s in the winter season and cool season, on the seventh lunar day of the bright fortnight of the month of M\'e4
gha, during the conjunction of Magh\'e4-nak\'f1atra and the full-moon day, and on the days when the moon is completely full, or not quite completely full, when these days are conjoined with the nak\'f1
atras from which the names of certain months are derived. One should also perform the \'e7r\'e4ddha ceremony on the twelfth lunar day when it is in conjunction with any of the nak\'f1atras named Anur\'e4dh\'e4, \'c7rava\'eba, Uttara-phalgun\'e9, Uttar\'e4
\'f1\'e4\'f2h\'e4 or Uttara-bh\'e4drapad\'e4. Again, one should perform this ceremony when the eleventh lunar day is in conjunction with either Uttara-phalgun\'e9, Uttar\'e4\'f1\'e4\'f2h\'e4 or Uttara-bh\'e4drapad\'e4. Finally, one
 should perform this ceremony on days conjoined with one's own birth star [janma-nak\'f1atra] or with \'c7rava\'eba-nak\'f1atra.
