\par }\pard\plain \s20\qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf18\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.15: Instructions for Civilized Human Beings
\par }\pard\plain \s330\qc \li0\ri0\sb540\sa540\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat10 \b\f35\fs36\cf8\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\insrsid12527637 CHAPTER FIFTEEN\line }{\insrsid30033 Instructions for Civilized Human Beings
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.15 Summary
\par }\pard\plain \qj \fi360\li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 The summary of the Fifteenth Chapter is as follows. In the previous chapter, \'c7r\'e9 N\'e4
rada Muni proved the importance of the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\insrsid30033  in society. Now, in this chapter, he will show the differences between different grades of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas.}{\insrsid30033  Among the }
{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas,}{\insrsid30033  some are householders and are mostly attached to fruitive activities or the betterment of social conditions. Above them, however, are }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\insrsid30033 
 who are very much attracted by austerities and penances and who retire from family life. They are known as }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 v\'e4naprasthas.}{\insrsid30033  Other }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\insrsid30033 
 are very much interested in studying the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 Vedas}{\insrsid30033  and explaining the purport of the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 Vedas}{\insrsid30033  to others. Such }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\insrsid30033  are called }
{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 brahmac\'e4r\'e9s.}{\insrsid30033  And still other }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\insrsid30033  are interested in different types of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 yoga,}{\insrsid30033  especially }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 
bhakti-yoga}{\insrsid30033  and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 j\'ef\'e4na-yoga.}{\insrsid30033  Such }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\insrsid30033  are mostly }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 sanny\'e4s\'e9s,}{\insrsid30033 
 members of the renounced order of life.
\par As far as householders are concerned, they engage in different types of scriptural activities, especially in offering oblations to their forefathers and giving as charity to other }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\insrsid30033 
 the paraphernalia engaged in such sacrifices. Generally the charity is given to }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 sanny\'e4s\'e9s, br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\insrsid30033  in the renounced order of life. If such a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 sanny\'e4s\'e9}{\insrsid30033 
 is not available, the charity is given to }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\insrsid30033  householders engaged in fruitive activities.
\par One should not make very elaborate arrangements to perform the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'e7r\'e4ddha}{\insrsid30033  ceremony of offering oblations to one's forefathers. The best process for the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'e7r\'e4ddha}{\insrsid30033 
 ceremony is to distribute }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 bh\'e4gavata-pras\'e4da}{\insrsid30033  (remnants of food that has first been offered to K\'e5\'f1\'eba) to all of one's forefathers and relatives. This makes a first-class }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'e7r
\'e4ddha}{\insrsid30033  ceremony. In the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'e7r\'e4ddha}{\insrsid30033  ceremony there is no need to offer meat or eat meat. 
Unnecessary killing of animals must be avoided. Those who are in the lower grades of society prefer to perform sacrifices by killing animals, but one who is advanced in knowledge must avoid such unnecessary violence.
\par }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 Br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\insrsid30033  should execute their regulative duties in worshiping Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu. Those who are advanced in knowledge of religious principles must avoid five kinds of irreligion, known as }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 vidharma, para-dharma, dharm\'e4bh\'e4sa, upadharma}{\insrsid30033  and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 chala-dharma.}{\insrsid30033  One must act according to the religious principles
 that suit his constitutional position; it is not that everyone must adhere to the same type of religion. A general principle is that a poor man should not unnecessarily endeavor for economic development. One who refrains from such endeavors but who engag
es in devotional service is most auspicious.
\par One who is not satisfied with the mind must fall to degradation. One must conquer lusty desires, anger, greed, fear, lamentation, illusion, fright, unnecessary talks on material subjects, violence, the four miser
ies of material existence, and the three material qualities. That is the objective of human life. One who has no faith in the spiritual master, who is identical with \'c7r\'e9 K\'e5\'f1\'eba, cannot get any benefit from reading }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 
\'e7\'e4stra.}{\insrsid30033  One should never consider the spiri
tual master an ordinary human being, even though the members of the spiritual master's family may think of him as such. Meditation and other processes of austerity are useful only if they help in advancement toward K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a consciousness; otherwise, they are simply a waste of time and labor. For those who are not devotees, such meditation and austerity cause falldown.
\par Every householder should be very careful because even though a householder may try to conquer the senses, he becomes a victim to the association of relatives and falls down. Thus a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 g\'e5hastha}{\insrsid30033  must become a }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 v\'e4naprastha}{\insrsid30033  or }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 sanny\'e4s\'e9,}{\insrsid30033  live in a secluded place, and be satisfied with food gotten by begging from door to door. He must chant the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 o\'e0k
\'e4ra mantra}{\insrsid30033  or Hare K\'e5\'f1\'eba }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 mantra,}{\insrsid30033  and in this way he will perceive transcendental bliss within himself. After taking }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 sanny\'e4sa,}{\insrsid30033 
 however, if one returns to }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 g\'e5hastha}{\insrsid30033  life, he is called a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 v\'e4nt\'e4\'e7\'e9,}{\insrsid30033 
 which means "one who eats his own vomit." Such a person is shameless. A householder should not give up the ritualistic ceremonies, and a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 sanny\'e4s\'e9}{\insrsid30033  should not live in society. If a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 
sanny\'e4s\'e9}{\insrsid30033  is agitated by the senses, he is a cheater influenced by the modes of passion and ignorance. When one assumes a role in goodness by starting philanthropic and altruistic activities, such activ
ities become impediments on the path of devotional service.
\par The best process for advancing in devotional service is to abide by the orders of the spiritual master, for only by his direction can one conquer the senses. Unless one is completely K\'e5\'f1\'eba conscio
us, there is a chance of falling down. Of course, in performing ritualistic ceremonies and other fruitive activities there are also many dangers at every moment. Fruitive activities have been divided into twelve portions. Because of performing fruitive ac
tivities, which are called the path of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 dharma,}{\insrsid30033  one has to accept the cycle of birth and death, but when one takes the path of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 mok\'f1a,}{\insrsid30033  or liberation, which is described in 
}{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\insrsid30033  as }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 arcan\'e4-m\'e4rga,}{\insrsid30033  one can get relief from the cycle of birth and death. The }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 Vedas}{\insrsid30033 
 describe these two paths as }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 pit\'e5-y\'e4na}{\insrsid30033  and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 deva-y\'e4na.}{\insrsid30033  Those who follow the paths of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 pit\'e5-y\'e4na}{\insrsid30033  and }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 deva-y\'e4na}{\insrsid30033  are never bewildered, even while in the material body. A monistic philosopher who gradually develops control of the senses understands that the objective of all the different }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 
\'e4\'e7ramas,}{\insrsid30033  the statuses of life, is salvation. One must live and act according to }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'e7\'e4stras.
\par }{\insrsid30033 If one who is performing the Vedic ritualistic ceremonies becomes a devotee, even if he is a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 g\'e5hastha,}{\insrsid30033  he can receive the causeless mercy of K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a. The objective of a devotee is to return home, back to Godhead. Such a devotee, even though not performing ritualistic ceremonies, advances in spiritual consciousness by the supreme will of the Personality of Godhead. One may actually become 
successful in spiritual consciousness by the mercy of devotees, or one may fall from spiritual consciousness by being disrespectful to devotees. In this regard, N\'e4
rada Muni narrated the history of how he had fallen from the Gandharva kingdom, how he was born in a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'e7\'fcdra}{\insrsid30033  family, and how by serving exalted }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\insrsid30033 
 he became the son of Lord Brahm\'e4 and was reinstated in his transcendental position. After narrating all these stories, N\'e4rada Muni praised the mercy received from the Lord by the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6
hira, after hearing from N\'e4rada, became ecstatic in love of K\'e5\'f1\'eba, and then N\'e4rada Muni left that place and returned to his own place. Thus \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, having described various descendants of the daughters of Dak\'f1
a, ends the Seventh Canto of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid30033 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.15.1
