\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid2757332 m\'e4\'e0 vij\'e4n\'e9y\'e4t}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 .}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  One should consider the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'e4c\'e4rya}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 to be as good as the Supreme Personality of Godhead. In spite of all these instructions, if one considers the spiritual master an ordinary human being, one is doomed. His study of the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Vedas}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 and his austerities and penances for enlightenment are all useless, like the bathing of an elephant. An elephant bathes in a lake quite thoroughly, but as soon as i
t comes on the shore it takes some dust from the ground and strews it over its body. Thus there is no meaning to the elephant's bath. One may argue by saying that since the spiritual master's relatives and the men of his neighborhood consider him an ordin
a
ry human being, what is the fault on the part of the disciple who considers the spiritual master an ordinary human being? This will be answered in the next verse, but the injunction is that the spiritual master should never be considered an ordinary man. 
One should strictly adhere to the instructions of the spiritual master, for if he is pleased, certainly the Supreme Personality of Godhead is pleased. }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Yasya pras\'e4d\'e4d bhagavat-pras\'e4do ya}{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid2757332 sy\'e4pras\'e4d\'e4n na gati\'f9 kuto 'pi}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 .
