red to the spokes of the chariot's wheels, and the top and bottom of the wheel itself are called religion and irreligion. The living entity in the bodily concept of life is the owner of the chariot. The Vedic mantra pra\'eb
ava is the bow, the pure living entity himself is the arrow, and the target is the Supreme Being.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid30033 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s537\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Ten kinds of life air always flow within the material body. They are called }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 pr\'e4\'eba, ap\'e4na, sam\'e4na, vy\'e4na, ud\'e4na, n\'e4ga, k\'fcrma, k\'e5kala, devadatta}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  and }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 dhana\'efjaya.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 They are compared here to t
he spokes of the chariot's wheels. The life air is the energy for all of a living being's activities, which are sometimes religious and sometimes irreligious. Thus religion and irreligion are said to be the upper and lower portions of the chariot's wheels
. When the living entity decides to go back home, back to Godhead, his target is Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Supreme Personality of Godhead. In the conditioned state of life, one does not understand that the goal of life is the Supreme Lord. }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Na te vidu\'f9 sv\'e4rtha-gati\'e0 hi vi\'f1\'ebu\'e0 dur\'e4\'e7ay\'e4 ye bahir-artha-m\'e4nina\'f9.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 The living entity tries to be happy within this material world, not understanding the target of his life. When he is purified, however, he gives up his bodily conception of life and his false identity a
s belonging to a certain community, a certain nation, a certain society, a certain family and so on (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 sarvop\'e4dhi-vinirmukta\'e0 tat-paratvena nirmalam}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
). Then he takes the arrow of his purified life, and with the help of the bow\emdash the transcendental chanting of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 pra\'ebava,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  or the Hare K\'e5\'f1\'eba }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 mantra}
{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \emdash he throws himself toward the Supreme Personality of Godhead.
\par }\pard\plain \s908\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'c7r\'e9la Vi\'e7van\'e4tha Cakravart\'e9 \'d6h\'e4
kura has commented that because the words "bow" and "arrow" are used in this verse, one might argue that the Su
preme Personality of Godhead and the living entity have become enemies. However, although the Supreme Personality of Godhead may become the so-called enemy of the living being, this is His chivalrous pleasure. For example, the Lord fought with Bh\'e9\'f1
ma, and when Bh\'e9\'f1ma pierced the Lord's body on the Battlefield of Kuruk\'f1
etra, this was a kind of humor or relationship, of which there are twelve. When the conditioned soul tries to reach the Lord by hurling an arrow at Him, the Lord takes pleasure, and the living
 entity gains the profit of going back home, back to Godhead. Another example given in this regard is that Arjuna, as a result of piercing the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'e4dh\'e4ra-m\'e9na,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  or the fish within the }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 cakra,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  achieved the valuable gain of Draupad\'e9. Similarly, if with the arrow of chanting the holy name of the Lord one pierces Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u's lotus feet, by dint of performing this heroic activity of devotional service one receives the benefit of returning home, back to Godhead.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.15.43, SB 7.15.44, SB 7.15.43-44
