\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa60\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 te ta\'e0 bhuktv\'e4 svarga-loka\'e0 vi\'e7\'e4la\'e0\line k\'f1\'e9\'ebe pu\'eb
ye martya-loka\'e0 vi\'e7anti\line eva\'e0 tray\'e9-dharmam anuprapann\'e4\line gat\'e4gata\'e0 k\'e4ma-k\'e4m\'e4 labhante
\par }\pard\plain \s135\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 "When those who follow the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 prav\'e5tti-m\'e4rga}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  have enjoyed heavenly sense pleasure, they return to this mortal planet again. Thus, through the Vedic principles, they achieve only flickering happiness." Following the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 prav\'e5tti-m\'e4rga,}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  the living entity who desires to be promoted to the higher planetary systems performs sacrifices regularly, and how he goes up and comes down again is described here in }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam,}
{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  as well as in }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  It is also said, }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 traigu\'ebya-vi\'f1ay\'e4 ved\'e4\'f9:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  "The }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Vedas}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  deal mainly with the three modes of material nature." The }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Vedas,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  especially three }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Vedas,}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  namely }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 S\'e4ma, Yajur}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  and }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'c5k,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 vividly describe this process of ascending to the higher planets and returning. But K\'e5\'f1\'eba advises Arjuna, }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 traigu\'ebya-vi\'f1ay\'e4 ved\'e4 nistraigu\'ebyo bhav\'e4rjuna:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 one has to transcend these three modes of material nature, and then one will be released from the cycle of birth and death. Otherwise, although one may be promoted to a higher planetary system such as Candraloka, one must again come down (}{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 k\'f1\'e9\'ebe pu\'ebye martya-loka\'e0 vi\'e7anti}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 ). After one's enjoyment due to pious ac
tivities is finished, one must return to this planet in rainfall and first take birth as a plant or creeper, which is eaten by various animals, including human beings, and turned to semen. This semen is injected into the female body, and thus the living e
ntity takes birth. Those who return to earth in this way take birth especially in higher families like those of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 br\'e4hma\'ebas.
\par }\pard\plain \s908\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
It may be remarked in this connection that even the modern so-called scientists who are going to the moon are not able to stay there, 
but are returning to their laboratories. Therefore, whether one goes to the moon by modern mechanical arrangements or by performing pious activities, one must return to earth. That is clearly stated in this verse and explained in }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  Even if one goes to the higher planetary systems (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid1531785 y\'e4nti deva-vrat\'e4 dev\'e4n}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
), one's place there is not secure; one must return to }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 martya-loka.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 \'c4brahma-bhuvan\'e4l lok\'e4\'f9 punar \'e4vartino 'rjuna:}{\cs1998\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033  }
{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 aside from the moon, even if one goes to Brahmaloka, one must return. }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 Ya\'e0 pr\'e4pya na nivartante tad dh\'e4ma parama\'e0 mama:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid30033 
 but if one goes back home, back to Godhead, he need not return to this material world.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid30033 SB 7.15.52
